messages:
lina: Opening a small docs thread for a migration + example cleanup: we need clearer guidance on choosing "envelope", "compact", or a middle-ground "envelope-lite" response shape. PRs incoming: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/478 (guide) and /479 (snippets). Looking for reviewers and a short ops logging example.

devon: +1. why "envelope-lite"? can you clarify tradeoffs? we should frame in terms of bytes/latency, type-safety, and routing checks.

lina: envelope-lite = keep typed header for routing/validation but allow a single freeform `body` string field for opaque payloads (smaller than full structured payload, still safe for routing). intended for places where full schema is heavy but you still want typed routing.

harry: sounds useful. nina in platform asked that any hybrid approach includes: (1) a `mode` enum in the API, (2) explicit `additionalProperties: false` for typed sections, and (3) a short fallback parsing example.

lina: draft canonical schema to include in docs/guides/function-calling.md (embed):

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "mode": { "type": "string", "enum": ["envelope", "compact", "envelope-lite"] },
    "structured_output": { "type": "boolean", "description": "hint for clients to expect typed content" },
    "header": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "route": { "type": "string" },
        "version": { "type": "string" }
      },
      "required": ["route"],
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
    "body": { "type": ["object", "string", "null"] }
  },
  "required": ["mode"],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

ops-bot: perf-note: envelope-lite reduces avg payload ~40% in our test for the billing-reporting flow (measured on 1k samples). Include link to perf artifact? (https://perf.redwood.ai/bench/178)

lina: adding a small parse strategy to the snippets section (docs/snippets/sdk). We should show typed-first parsing (if mode==envelope) then body-as-json, otherwise compact attempt, then final fuzzy heuristics. Example Python snippet: 

```py
# typed-first fallback strategy
if resp_json.get("mode") == "envelope":
    try:
        parsed = validate_header_then_body(resp_json)
    except ValidationError:
        parsed = heuristics_parse(resp_text)
else:
    try:
        parsed = parse_compact(resp_text)
    except ParseError:
        parsed = heuristics_parse(resp_text)
```

and a minimal TypeScript example for the SDK: 

```ts
// mode-aware parser
if ((resp as any).mode === 'envelope') {
  try {
    return parseEnvelope(resp);
  } catch (e) {
    return fallbackHeuristic(respText);
  }
} else {
  try {
    return parseCompact(respText);
  } catch (e) {
    return fallbackHeuristic(respText);
  }
}
```

qa: tiny test note: add a unit test that injects partial outputs (body=null, truncated json) so SDKs exercise heuristics. I can add test cases in PR 479.

devon: naming nit: across the docs we have "structured_output", "structuredOutput", and "structured_response" floating around. Can we standardize on `structured_output` (snake_case)? That's what our API surface uses.

lina: yes, will normalize to `structured_output`. Also will add a one-paragraph "when to use" table: envelope=best for routing & strict validation; compact=best for tiny payloads & speed; envelope-lite=compromise for typed routing with small opaque payload.

ops-bot: request: include a sample log line for ops that shows routing decision and parser fallback, e.g.:

LOG: SERVICE=redwood-api route=users.billing mode=envelope-lite parser=validate_header_then_body fallback=heuristics_parse latency_ms=24

lina: will add that log example and call out that teams should emit `mode` & `route` for observability.

harry: I'll review PR 478 tonight and sanity-check the schema text and perf callout. qa -> great, please add the unit tests. Target merge: Mon EOD. :white_check_mark:
